# Pull request style

Pull requests are the context trail for people and AI agents who read this repo later. Keep every one clear, simple, and short.

- **Title:** plain words that say what changed. No "N of M" numbering or totals.
- **Body:** under about 200 words, in this order:
  - **Why:** one or two sentences on the problem this solves.
  - **What changed:** 3 to 6 bullets, one per file or idea.
  - **How it was checked:** what you ran and what happened.
  - **Open questions:** only if there are any.
- Write short, full sentences in plain words. Explain any label or acronym the first time it appears.
- **Keep it small.** One script, rule, or skill per pull request, plus its test. Aim for under about 200 changed lines. If it needs a long explanation, split it.
- Small pull requests make debugging easier, because a problem points to one change. Fixes found later get their own pull request; don't fold them into unrelated work.
- Refer to other pull requests by number (#3), not by their place in a plan.
